From 9ed611d95c2bd08119b5c454327ff42b330d4277 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?J=C3=B3n=20Bjarni=20Bjarnason?= <jon@centroid.is>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 10:22:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] input: re-take a cursor surface another pointer has released

A client may pass the same wl_surface to wl_pointer.set_cursor on the
wl_pointer of more than one seat. flutter-elinux does exactly that: it
keeps one cursor surface and sets it on whichever pointer entered last.
The surface has a single committed hook, so committed_private names one
pointer, the last one to set it, while every pointer that set it keeps a
sprite view of it.

When one of those pointers drops its sprite -- its seat loses the
pointer capability (a VNC client disconnects), it sets another surface
because it entered another client, or it sets a NULL cursor --
pointer_unmap_sprite() clears the surface's committed and
committed_private regardless of which pointer they belong to. The other
pointer still has pointer->sprite->surface == surface. Its next
set_cursor with the same surface and a different hotspot (the client
changing cursor shape) skips the role setup, because the sprite already
shows that surface, and calls pointer_cursor_surface_committed()
directly, which reads pointer->sprite through the NULL
committed_private:

  segfault at 90 ... in libweston-16.so.0.0.0[3ebd2]
  #0 pointer_cursor_surface_committed (input.c:3584)
  #1 pointer_set_cursor (input.c:3662)

Treat a sprite whose surface this pointer does not own as no sprite:
unmap it and set the surface up again, which makes this pointer the
owner. Both the early return for an unchanged cursor and the setup
branch now check ownership.
---
 libweston/input.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/libweston/input.c b/libweston/input.c
index 3be879bb..2851f5d3 100644
--- a/libweston/input.c
+++ b/libweston/input.c
@@ -3631,11 +3631,18 @@ pointer_set_cursor(struct wl_client *client, struct wl_resource *resource,
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/* A client may hand the same wl_surface to the wl_pointer of more
+	 * than one seat. The surface has one committed hook, so only the
+	 * pointer in committed_private owns it; another pointer can unmap
+	 * the sprite and clear that hook while this one still shows the
+	 * surface. Take the surface back instead of trusting the sprite. */
 	if (pointer->sprite && pointer->sprite->surface == surface &&
+	    surface->committed_private == pointer &&
 	    pointer->hotspot.c.x == x && pointer->hotspot.c.y == y)
 		return;
 
-	if (!pointer->sprite || pointer->sprite->surface != surface) {
+	if (!pointer->sprite || pointer->sprite->surface != surface ||
+	    surface->committed_private != pointer) {
 		if (weston_surface_set_role(surface, "wl_pointer-cursor",
 					    resource,
 					    WL_POINTER_ERROR_ROLE) < 0)
